# Infrastructure — AWS ECS Fargate (Terraform)

Everything needed to run **{{projectName}}** on AWS:

- **Networking** — a small public VPC (2 subnets, no NAT gateways: cost-lean).
- **ALB** — path-routes `/api/*`, `/admin/*`, `/django-static/*` to the Django
  backend and everything else to the nginx frontend. Optional custom domain
  (ACM certificate + Route53 alias, HTTP→HTTPS redirect).
- **ECS Fargate** — `backend` (gunicorn, port 8000) and `frontend` (nginx,
  port 80) services with deployment circuit breakers{{#if withCelery}}, plus a Celery `worker`{{#if withCeleryBeat}} and `beat`{{/if}} service{{/if}}.
- **RDS PostgreSQL** — always provisioned (production parity, even when local
  dev runs SQLite). Credentials generated by Terraform and injected from
  **Secrets Manager** (`DATABASE_URL`, `DJANGO_SECRET_KEY`).
{{#if withS3}}
- **S3 media bucket** — the `files` app stores uploads here (no MinIO in AWS;
  `AWS_S3_ENDPOINT_URL` stays unset in production).
{{/if}}
{{#if withRedis}}
- **ElastiCache Redis** — Celery broker/result backend.
{{/if}}
- **Migrations** — a dedicated one-off task definition; run it with
  `dude iac migrate --env <env>` after every deploy that changes models.

State lives in S3 + DynamoDB (created once by `bootstrap`, shared by all
environments); each environment has its own state key under
`terraform/environments/<env>/`.

## First deployment

```bash
dude iac login --env dev                                        # AWS credentials (SSO or keys)
dude iac bootstrap --state-bucket-prefix <your-org> --env dev --yes   # state backend + ECR repos (once)
dude iac init --env dev                                         # terraform init against the S3 backend
dude iac apply --env dev                                        # provision the infrastructure
dude iac ship --env dev                                         # build + push + deploy both images
dude iac migrate --env dev                                      # apply Django migrations
dude iac output --env dev                                       # app_url, ECR URLs, RDS endpoint…
```

## Day-to-day

```bash
dude iac ship --env dev            # deploy the current commit (tag = git SHA)
dude iac migrate --env dev         # after model changes
dude iac status --env dev          # ECS service health (--service backend|frontend{{#if withCelery}}|worker{{/if}}{{#if withCeleryBeat}}|beat{{/if}})
dude iac logs --env dev --follow   # CloudWatch logs (--service …)
dude iac plan --env dev            # preview infra changes
```

## More environments

```bash
dude iac new-env --env staging --from dev
dude iac init --env staging && dude iac apply --env staging
```

## Custom domain

Set `domain_name` and `route53_zone_name` in
`terraform/environments/<env>/terraform.tfvars`, then `dude iac apply`.
Terraform requests an ACM certificate (DNS-validated), switches the ALB to
HTTPS and creates the alias record.

## Teardown

```bash
dude iac destroy --env dev         # helm-free: terraform destroy + backend cleanup
```

The shared bootstrap (state bucket, lock table, ECR repos) is destroyed only
when no other environment still uses it — pass `--keep-backend` to force-keep.

> All terraform invocations run inside a pinned Docker runner
> (`iac/runner/Dockerfile`); `aws`, `docker` and `git` stay on the host.
> Override with `DUDE_IAC_RUNNER=host`.
